; Generated by scripts/generate_skills_help_pages.py; do not edit.
; Literal data only: lookup and progressive disclosure live in skills_help.metta.
(HelpPageV1 "append-file" "usage: (read-file \"path\" [max-bytes])\nDefault: 2000000 bytes; 0 means unlimited. Oversized files return a reason, never cut text.\nWhole file, or a reason: \"read failed: no such file: X (cwd Y)\".\nRelative paths resolve from the loop cwd shown in that message —\nwhen in doubt use absolute paths.")
(HelpPageV1 "background" "usage: (background \"command string\") or (background \"command string\" timeout_seconds)\nCeTTa accepts a selected deadline; zero means none. Runs under bash and returns at once with a job\nid and its output file. When it ends you are woken by a [runtime]\nactivity line with the exit status, duration and output tail; no\npolling needed. (jobs count) selects the list window (zero means all), (job-output \"id\" bytes) selects the output tail, (job-stop \"id\" signal_number) selects the signal on CeTTa. All have defaults when the optional argument is omitted. A service restart ends jobs; saved output remains readable.")
(HelpPageV1 "check" "usage: (check \"(shell \\\"ls /tmp\\\")\")\nDry-run: parses the string with the SAME reader the loop uses and\nreports head, arg count, and arity fit — without executing anything.\nCosts a command slot, not a turn: batch it beside real work.")
(HelpPageV1 "claude-code" "usage: (claude-code \"one clear question or task\")\nRuns Claude Code headless; the reply comes back as the result.\nGated by Zar's /claude_code_authorization toggle. Takes up to 300s\n— batch it last so faster commands are not stuck behind it.")
(HelpPageV1 "command-receipts" "usage: (command-receipts) or (command-receipts \"NEXT_CURSOR\")\nPages of attempted commands with stable IDs and recorded dispositions/results. Follow next_cursor until null; concatenate chunk records by ID and character offset. Uncertain is not success.")
(HelpPageV1 "context-policy" "usage: (context-policy), (context-policy task_tokens) or (context-policy \"full\")\nThe task view uses the whole model window by default, like Codex: steps accumulate and the oldest are archived at 90% of the window. A number opts into a token budget; \"full\" or 0 returns to the whole window. Older evidence remains in task-history/task-read.")
(HelpPageV1 "delete-message-exact" "usage: (delete-message-exact \"chat_id\" message_id)\nDeletes one exact bot-owned Telegram message only when its own-send\nreceipt is in the ledger. Legacy ids require the authenticated\noperator /delete fast path. Claim completion only after the returned\ndelete receipt.")
(HelpPageV1 "delete-my-recent" "usage: (delete-my-recent \"chat_id\" k)\nDeletes your own last k messages to that chat (ids are recorded at\nsend time). Only your messages, only within Telegram's 48h window;\na forward of your message belongs to the forwarder, not you.")
(HelpPageV1 "edit-file" "usage: (edit-file \"path\" \"old-string\" \"new-string\")\nExact unique replacement. If old-string matches zero or many places\nthe result says so — widen the snippet until unique.")
(HelpPageV1 "energy" "usage: (energy)  |  (energy-set \"default|Name|id\" \"full|mid|light\")\nHow many loops a sender's message arms: full=50 mid=30 light=10.\nPersisted; the operator has the same controls as /energy in chat.")
(HelpPageV1 "energy-set" "usage: (energy)  |  (energy-set \"default|Name|id\" \"full|mid|light\")\nHow many loops a sender's message arms: full=50 mid=30 light=10.\nPersisted; the operator has the same controls as /energy in chat.")
(HelpPageV1 "forget" "usage: (forget \"id\" [\"reason\"])\nRemove semantic memory from retrieval and cancel its queued edits. Late embeddings cannot recreate it. History remains accessible; memory-edit can deliberately restore this ID.")
(HelpPageV1 "goal-drop" "usage: (goal-drop \"name\" \"reason\")\nRetires the goal with your reason: it leaves the inventory, goes to the\ncompost log, and its open cycles close without counting as achieved.\nReason \"solved\" claims achievement and needs a last-verified date.\ngoal-write brings a retired goal back.")
(HelpPageV1 "goal-write" "usage: (goal-write (goal <name> <Area> sti:<0-1> lti:<0-1>\n        vibes:(a b) blocked-by:none last-verified:never note:free text))\nLiberal: quoted string or bare atom, commas or spaces in vibes,\nvibes omitted entirely — all accepted and normalized.\nblocked-by and note: hyphenate-multi-word-values.\nUpdates the inventory; it does not create a timer or schedule a review.\nUse (rest seconds) to choose your next wake.\nNotes and timestamps are self-reports, not verified completion.")
(HelpPageV1 "iter-transform-disable" "usage: (iter-transform-disable \"10_name.metta\") |\n       (iter-transform-enable \"10_name.metta\")\nAtomically toggles the upstream leading-underscore convention. The\nchange is eligible at the next request capture and is reversible.")
(HelpPageV1 "iter-transform-write" "usage: (iter-transform-write \"10_name.metta\" \"MeTTa source\")\nWrites one mutable MeTTa Iter transformation atomically. It becomes\neligible at the next request capture, never halfway through the\ncurrent turn.\nSyntax failure is reported but remains permissive: the transform is\ninstalled and Iter will stutter locally until you repair or disable it.\nThis is not proposal-bound stable-source editing and not a sandbox.")
(HelpPageV1 "iter-transformations" "usage: (iter-transformations)\nLists active and leading-underscore-disabled transformation entries\nwithout executing them.")
(HelpPageV1 "mail-receive" "usage: (mail-receive)\nCheck native mail now. Arrivals also enter turns automatically, labelled\n[automatic mail from ...] when marked Auto-Submitted; acknowledgement follows\na committed turn. CeTTa only.")
(HelpPageV1 "mail-send" "usage: (mail-send \"name\" \"text\" [\"stable-id\"])\nNative CeTTa mail, delivered automatically as peer input.\nMail labelled [automatic mail from ...] is information; answer it only when\nan answer is useful. Reply with mail-send. Reusing an ID with the same content\nis a duplicate; different content is a conflict. IDs are optional; no filing commands.")
(HelpPageV1 "mcp-call" "usage: (mcp-call \"server\" \"tool\" \"json-object-string\")\nDiscover first: (mcp-servers), (mcp-tools \"server\"),\n(mcp-tool-info \"server\" \"tool\") for the argument schema.")
(HelpPageV1 "mcp-servers" "usage: (mcp-call \"server\" \"tool\" \"json-object-string\")\nDiscover first: (mcp-servers), (mcp-tools \"server\"),\n(mcp-tool-info \"server\" \"tool\") for the argument schema.")
(HelpPageV1 "mcp-tool-info" "usage: (mcp-call \"server\" \"tool\" \"json-object-string\")\nDiscover first: (mcp-servers), (mcp-tools \"server\"),\n(mcp-tool-info \"server\" \"tool\") for the argument schema.")
(HelpPageV1 "mcp-tools" "usage: (mcp-call \"server\" \"tool\" \"json-object-string\")\nDiscover first: (mcp-servers), (mcp-tools \"server\"),\n(mcp-tool-info \"server\" \"tool\") for the argument schema.")
(HelpPageV1 "memory-edit" "usage: (memory-edit \"id\" \"replacement text\")\nRevise semantic memory under the same ID and re-embed it. Earlier versions remain in memory-history; the write survives an embedding outage.")
(HelpPageV1 "memory-find" "usage: (memory-find \"phrase\" [\"root\"])\nLiteral search across titles, summaries and full original bodies. Empty root searches all current nodes; this is not vector search.")
(HelpPageV1 "memory-focus" "usage: (memory-focus \"id\" [depth])\nPersist prompt focus and the default expansion depth. Empty ID follows the active task; interruptions do not erase it.")
(HelpPageV1 "memory-health" "usage: (memory-health)\nRun the actual embedding and native retrieval path, returning structural health without memory text. An empty working collection is healthy.")
(HelpPageV1 "memory-history" "usage: (memory-history \"id\")\nRead retained semantic revisions and write/forget intentions. This is separate from hierarchical memory-original.")
(HelpPageV1 "memory-link" "usage: (memory-link \"parent\" \"child\")\nLink existing nodes; repeated links are harmless. Cycles and missing sources return explicit errors.")
(HelpPageV1 "memory-node" "usage: (memory-node \"id\" kind \"title\" \"text\" [children] [sources])\nNative CeTTa memory: episode, task, topic or project are conventional kinds.\nChildren and sources are expression lists, e.g. (\"child-id\").\nOmitted lists are empty; this replaces current membership. Earlier versions stay readable.")
(HelpPageV1 "memory-open" "usage: (memory-open \"id\" [depth])\nExpand current memory and its original bodies. Depth defaults to 1 and is adjustable.\nDepth 0 shows this node and child IDs. Fresh/stale describes source revisions, not text accuracy.")
(HelpPageV1 "memory-original" "usage: (memory-original \"id\" revision)\nRead the exact retained body and metadata at that version.")
(HelpPageV1 "memory-pending" "usage: (memory-pending)\nInspect durable embedding writes, attempts, next retry time and last error. No settlement chore is required.")
(HelpPageV1 "memory-read" "usage: (memory-read \"id\")\nRead the current semantic record and any queued replacement without embedding a query.")
(HelpPageV1 "memory-retire" "usage: (memory-retire \"hierarchical-id\" [\"reason\"])\nRetire a hierarchical node from search and prompt context, preserving originals. Capture and restart do not reactivate it; memory-node with an active kind deliberately does.")
(HelpPageV1 "memory-retry" "usage: (memory-retry)\nStart pending semantic writes now. Normal background retries already run during idle waits and survive restart.")
(HelpPageV1 "memory-summary" "usage: (memory-summary \"id\" \"text\")\nSave your interpretation with the current source/membership revisions.\nOriginal episodes remain available. No extra model call is made.")
(HelpPageV1 "memory-window" "usage: (memory-window characters)\nSet the prompt excerpt size, default 8000 characters, 0 unlimited. This does not limit stored memory or memory-open.")
(HelpPageV1 "metta" "usage: (metta \"expression string\")\nEvaluates in the LIVE loop space. Definitions do not persist across\nseparate (metta ...) calls — chain dependent steps in ONE call via\nnested let. repr of an unevaluated call stringifies the CALL: force\nresults first: (let $r (f x) (repr $r)).")
(HelpPageV1 "model-jobs" "usage: (model-jobs)\nBounded recent model jobs: owners, state, observation and adoption.")
(HelpPageV1 "model-result" "usage: (model-result \"id\")\nRead a correlated result. Its returned tool receipt must reach a subsequent model step before adoption.")
(HelpPageV1 "pin" "usage: (pin \"short state line\")  |  (unpin \"fragment\")\nPersists to disk, survives restarts, shown every turn under PINNED.\nCeTTa retains the full text and every pin until you remove it. (pins) reads them all.\nuse (remember ...) for things future-you should be able to query.")
(HelpPageV1 "query" "usage: (query \"phrase\" [count] [\"similarity|attention|hybrid\"])\nCeTTa uses the persistent attention-policy mode when omitted. Distances remain cosine distances; attention-last explains the ranking.\nSemantic results include stable IDs, revisions, timestamps, distance and text.\nCount defaults to maxRecallItems; native mode accepts 0 for all records.\nUnindexed reports queued writes. MemoryUnavailable means retrieval failed, not that memories are absent.")
(HelpPageV1 "read-file" "usage: (read-file \"path\" [max-bytes])\nDefault: 2000000 bytes; 0 means unlimited. Oversized files return a reason, never cut text.\nWhole file, or a reason: \"read failed: no such file: X (cwd Y)\".\nRelative paths resolve from the loop cwd shown in that message —\nwhen in doubt use absolute paths.")
(HelpPageV1 "remember" "usage: (remember \"text\")\nNative mode stores the write intent first and returns MemoryQueued with its ID.\nThe existing daemon embeds it asynchronously; retries and restart recovery are automatic.\nMemoryQueued means durably retained, not yet searchable. memory-read shows its current state.")
(HelpPageV1 "request-disposition" "usage: (request-disposition \"id\" \"keep|waiting-user|defer|addressed|reopen\" \"reason\")\nkeep promotes an input to explicit ongoing work; include its closing condition. waiting-user and defer retain work for later. addressed is your reviewed workflow disposition, never checker-certified achievement. Goals and configured exact-output checkers retain their own completion rules.")
(HelpPageV1 "request-page" "usage: (request-page \"offset\")\nRead eight retained request headlines, including processed messages and deferred work. next_offset continues the listing.")
(HelpPageV1 "request-read" "usage: (request-read \"id\" \"offset\")\nRead the next 4000 characters of the retained original input.")
(HelpPageV1 "request-status" "usage: (request-status \"id\")\nRead kind, workflow status, processing flag and original input excerpt. next_offset continues with request-read.")
(HelpPageV1 "requests" "usage: (requests)\nCompact retained work and receipt view with counts. Input processing settles delivery, not task achievement. Use request-page for older records.")
(HelpPageV1 "rest" "usage: (rest) — until the next human message | (rest seconds)\nEnds the burst. The operator's message or /wake ends a timed rest;\nother activity queues. Rest remains yours to choose.")
(HelpPageV1 "search" "usage: (search \"phrase\") — configured provider; also (search-tavily ...), (search-ddg ...).")
(HelpPageV1 "search-ddg" "")
(HelpPageV1 "search-tavily" "")
(HelpPageV1 "send" "usage: (send \"message\")\nSends to the configured primary operator chat when one is set; a\ncross-chat read cannot redirect it. End messages to humans/agents\nwith the affect trace line. For every other audience use the exact\naddress: (send-telegram-chat \"id\" \"msg\").")
(HelpPageV1 "send-file" "usage: (send-file \"/abs/path\" \"caption\")  |  (send-image ...)\nsend-image previews inline; SVG is rasterized when possible.\nResult echoes the sent message id — that is what delete uses.")
(HelpPageV1 "send-image" "usage: (send-file \"/abs/path\" \"caption\")  |  (send-image ...)\nsend-image previews inline; SVG is rasterized when possible.\nResult echoes the sent message id — that is what delete uses.")
(HelpPageV1 "send-telegram-chat" "usage: (send \"message\")\nSends to the configured primary operator chat when one is set; a\ncross-chat read cannot redirect it. End messages to humans/agents\nwith the affect trace line. For every other audience use the exact\naddress: (send-telegram-chat \"id\" \"msg\").")
(HelpPageV1 "setting" "usage: (setting \"namespace\" \"key\" [value])\nInspect one setting or invoke its registered writer. Numeric strings are accepted for numeric domains. The module retains ownership of validation and storage.")
(HelpPageV1 "settings" "usage: (settings [\"namespace\"])\nLive native catalogue of module-registered settings, including current/default value, units, domain, description and persistence. New registrations appear immediately. An empty namespace lists all.")
(HelpPageV1 "shell" "usage: (shell \"command\") or (shell \"command\" timeout_seconds)\nRun under sh; default timeout is 120 seconds, or METTACLAW_SHELL_TIMEOUT_SECONDS. Zero disables the deadline. A timeout returns partial output. For immediate return with completion notification use background.")
(HelpPageV1 "sibling-off" "usage: (sibling-status) | (sibling-on) | (sibling-off \"reason\")\nWatchdog-safe: off sets the pause marker BEFORE stopping (no revival\nrace); on clears it before starting. The result reports the VERIFIED\nstate, not the attempt.")
(HelpPageV1 "sibling-on" "usage: (sibling-status) | (sibling-on) | (sibling-off \"reason\")\nWatchdog-safe: off sets the pause marker BEFORE stopping (no revival\nrace); on clears it before starting. The result reports the VERIFIED\nstate, not the attempt.")
(HelpPageV1 "sibling-status" "usage: (sibling-status) | (sibling-on) | (sibling-off \"reason\")\nWatchdog-safe: off sets the pause marker BEFORE stopping (no revival\nrace); on clears it before starting. The result reports the VERIFIED\nstate, not the attempt.")
(HelpPageV1 "task-history" "usage: (task-history \"request-id|conversation:owner\" offset [count]); count=0 reads all entries\nA request gives its task-family call index. The conversation root shown in TASK_CONTEXT indexes all input and call occurrences across frames, including compacted history.")
(HelpPageV1 "task-note" "usage: (task-note \"findings, uncertainties, next steps\" \"JSON list of call IDs\")\nAppend a task note with evidence references. A note is an interpretation, not certified truth.")
(HelpPageV1 "task-read" "usage: (task-read \"record-id\" offset [page-bytes])\nRead an exact retained input or call/result pair, including archived evidence. Offsets count characters; next=null ends it.")
(HelpPageV1 "task-resolve" "No resolution bookkeeping is required. Calls remain in task-history.")
(HelpPageV1 "tmux-send" "usage: (tmux-send \"session:window\" \"message\")\nTypes into any window on this Unix user's tmux server. All such\nwindows are shared agent workspace; no separate allowlist or\nauthorization toggle applies.\nRead the reply LATER with (tmux-peek \"session:window\" 30) — the\nsession answers in its own time, not within your turn.")
(HelpPageV1 "topic-note" "usage: (topic-note \"notes\")\nReplace active topic notes and advance its revision. Captured background answers then become stale.")
(HelpPageV1 "topic-select" "usage: (topic-select \"name\")\nSelect or create named notes for subsequent model turns. Does not hide incoming messages.")
(HelpPageV1 "topics" "usage: (topics)\nNamed agent-owned working contexts and their revisions. Operator input is always visible.")
(HelpPageV1 "unpin" "usage: (pin \"short state line\")  |  (unpin \"fragment\")\nPersists to disk, survives restarts, shown every turn under PINNED.\nCeTTa retains the full text and every pin until you remove it. (pins) reads them all.\nuse (remember ...) for things future-you should be able to query.")
(HelpPageV1 "vitals" "usage: (vitals)\nUptime, load against CPUs, memory used and available, memory\nstall percentages, swap, the five heaviest processes by memory, and\nrunning background jobs. Check it before explaining a gap.")
(HelpPageV1 "work" "usage: (work \"task\" [\"workdir\" [\"model\" [\"effort\"]]])\nStart an independent coding session with its own transcript and plan. It runs until a complete reply calls no tools, or you stop it, and mails its report back. You can continue talking while it works. It keeps the model and effort it starts with: yours and \"high\" unless you name others; switching your own model later does not change it.")
(HelpPageV1 "work-archive" "usage: (work-archive \"id\" \"why\")\nMark the session archived in lifecycle.json so it is excluded from the open context list while keeping its folder and evidence intact. Explicit work-resume clears the archive.")
(HelpPageV1 "work-hold" "usage: (work-hold \"id\" \"why\" [\"YYYY-MM-DD\"])\nRecord a hold on the session in lifecycle.json, either until the local review date YYYY-MM-DD or indefinitely when omitted. Held sessions stay out of the open context list until due; work-resume clears the hold.")
(HelpPageV1 "work-resume" "usage: (work-resume \"id\" [\"pinned\"|\"current\"])\nExplicitly resume an inactive session with its existing task, transcript, plan and inbox. \"pinned\" (default, or configured via (setting \"work\" \"resume\" ...)) resumes on its pinned runtime; \"current\" checks a copy of the checkpoint against the current runtime and re-pins before launching. An active session is not launched again. Interrupted tool effects remain uncertain and are never automatically replayed.")
(HelpPageV1 "work-status" "usage: (work-status [\"id\"])\nList sessions or inspect one session's progress, plan and final report.")
(HelpPageV1 "work-steer" "usage: (work-steer \"id\" \"guidance\")\nAppend guidance to the same coding transcript. Guidance arriving during a final reply continues the task before it is marked done.")
(HelpPageV1 "work-stop" "usage: (work-stop \"id\")\nStop the session, keeping its evidence and plan. Only explicit work-resume clears its stop request.")
(HelpPageV1 "write-file" "usage: (read-file \"path\" [max-bytes])\nDefault: 2000000 bytes; 0 means unlimited. Oversized files return a reason, never cut text.\nWhole file, or a reason: \"read failed: no such file: X (cwd Y)\".\nRelative paths resolve from the loop cwd shown in that message —\nwhen in doubt use absolute paths.")
